#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054345 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0448a1c0-e35b-56a1-aaf3-6e7a33962ade" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Set Defined Over a Finite or Infinite Set"}] :question/id #uuid "64808f4a-311c-52d8-bd9e-bfa88a4d3ffc" :question/math-academy-id "e-13142" :question/problem "Consider the set $A$, defined as\n\n$A = \\left \\{4^n: n \\in \\mathbb Z,\\, -2\\leq n\\leq 2 \\right \\}$.\n\nDescribe $A$ by listing all of its elements." :question/worked-solution "The condition after the \"$$:\" symbol,\n\n$$\nn \\in \\mathbb Z,\\, -2\\leq n\\leq 2\n$$\n\nstates that $n$ can be any integer that satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-2\\leq n\\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence $a_n$, given by\n\n$$\na_n = 4^n, \\qquad n \\in \\{0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2\\}\n$$\n\nLet's write down all the terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{-2} &= 4^{-2} = \\frac{1}{16} \\\\\na_{-1} &= 4^{-1} = \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\na_{0} &= 4^{0} = 1 \\\\\na_{1} &= 4^{1} = 4 \\\\\na_{2} &= 4^{2} = 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{16},\\,\\dfrac{1}{4},\\,1,\\,4,\\,16 \\right\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054354 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e298f475-c23a-5f79-bdef-25f46f0b96dc" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Set Defined Over a Special Set"}] :question/id #uuid "db0a407c-cfd4-5273-8bc0-8ccfd91348a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13177" :question/problem "Consider the set $A$, defined as\n$A = \\{(-1)^n \\, n: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\}$.\nWrite down a set that's equivalent to $A$ using ellipsis notation." :question/worked-solution "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence $a_n$, given by\n\n$$\na_n = (-1)^n \\,n, \\qquad n \\in \\mathbb N = \\{1,2,3,4,\\ldots\\}\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= (-1)^{1}(1) =-1 \\\\\na_{2} &= (-1)^{2}(2) = 2 \\\\\na_{3} &= (-1)^{3}(3) =-3 \\\\\na_{4} &= (-1)^{4}(4) = 4 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{-1, 2, -3, 4, \\ldots \\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054363 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e28c42e-0d82-5e82-a130-a2cb72fb96d1" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Set Defined Using a Recursive Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "25f3404c-ceff-5fef-9b34-167ce58121c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13285" :question/problem "Consider the set $A$, defined as\n\n$A = \\big \\{a_n \\,:\\,a_{n+1} = 2a_{n} - 1, \\, a_1 = 2,\\, n \\in \\mathbb N \\big\\}$.\n\nWrite down a set that's equivalent to $A$ using ellipsis notation." :question/worked-solution "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence $a_n$, given by\n\n$$\na_{n+1} =2a_{n} - 1, \\quad a_1 =2,\\qquad n \\in \\mathbb N\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 2 \\\\\na_{2} &= 2a_{1} - 1 \\\\\n&= 2(2) - 1 \\\\\n&= 3 \\\\\na_{3} &= 2a_{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= 2(3) - 1 \\\\\n&= 5 \\\\\na_{4} &= 2a_{3} - 1 \\\\\n&= 2(5) - 1 \\\\\n&= 9 \\\\\na_{5} &= 2a_{4} - 1 \\\\\n&= 2(9) - 1 \\\\\n&= 17 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{2,3,5,9,17,\\ldots \\right\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e298f475-c23a-5f79-bdef-25f46f0b96dc" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Set Defined Over a Special Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b66c063-a855-5e29-83d7-f99343ea66a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0, ± 1, ± 8, ± 27, …}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051796} {:answer/id #uuid "9d224032-94b4-5877-9004-a3fb767a6edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0, ± 1, ± 2, ± 3, …}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051797} {:answer/id #uuid "bd135c9c-51c1-56de-bcc6-def4e30477bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0, ± 1, ± 4, ± 9, …}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051798} {:answer/id #uuid "038f26ff-6cab-59f3-80d8-d65fed0a92ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0, 3, 9, 27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051799} {:answer/id #uuid "6093173f-ec54-57d6-ad8e-f5e9957ab180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0, 1, 8, 27,...}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b66c063-a855-5e29-83d7-f99343ea66a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0, ± 1, ± 8, ± 27, …}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051796} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ce60621-5393-53c9-90f0-927be405ccad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051795}] :question/id #uuid "ed160dbd-f540-5634-be7f-766ba6860cbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-180740" :question/problem "Consider the set $A$, defined as\n\n$A = {n^{3}: n ∈ Z}$.\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0448a1c0-e35b-56a1-aaf3-6e7a33962ade" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Set Defined Over a Finite or Infinite Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d837ad9-7679-54da-bb2f-e46e38aae79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8, 15, 16, 25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051897} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4a4c10-b3bc-54a2-ae1b-74f374cfcf7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8, 15, 24, 35, …}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051898} {:answer/id #uuid "f7968f07-203f-5c14-867a-1004d7b72728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8, 15, 24, 45…}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051899} {:answer/id #uuid "45ac8df5-39ea-5350-9b65-827c02b4efa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8, 12, 18, 24, …}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051900} {:answer/id #uuid "516dd241-b576-531d-94bb-4853576404b9" :answer/typ